(* dyncond3.rwhile -- a DYNAMIC CONDITIONAL and nothing else: no loop, two
 * variables, so ri_fp3 (which dispatches over exactly V0/V1/V2) can interpret it.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS (2026-08-09).  It is the control experiment that names the wall
 * in front of the jones-self battery.  examples/reverse.rwhile cannot be
 * specialised through ri_fp3 -- spec_av dies with `'error <= '41` -- and the
 * obvious reading is "loops are not supported yet".  That reading is wrong:
 * loop_static2/loop_static3 ARE loops and they specialise fine, while THIS
 * program has no loop at all and dies with exactly the same '41.
 *
 * So the boundary is DYNAMIC CONTROL, not loops.  A dynamic branch in the
 * interpreted program makes spec_av dynamicize the store; ri_fp3's code stack
 * Cd is in that store; ri_fp3's own main loop is then a loop whose exit test
 * `=? Cd nil` stops being static in the middle of being unrolled, and spec_av's
 * 'lcheck has no residualisation path for that.  See
 * FINDINGS_reversible_projections.md section 10 and the `jones-self-open` tests.
 *
 * [dyncond3](d) = ('one . d) for non-nil d, ('two . nil) for d = nil.
 * The input is carried into the output because R-WHILE's store invariant leaves
 * no other way to consume it. *)
read V0;
  if V0 then
    V1 ^= 'one
  else
    V1 ^= 'two
  fi (=? V1 'one);
  V0 <= cons V1 V0;
write V0
